Setting Up Kali Linux in VirtualBox (Step-by-Step)
1. Prerequisites
Before starting, ensure you have: A system with at least:
8 GB RAM (4 GB minimum)
50 GB free disk space
Virtualization enabled in BIOS/UEFI
Installed:
Oracle VM VirtualBox
Kali Linux ISO file
2. Download Required Files
a. Download VirtualBox  and Go to: https://www.virtualbox.org/⁠
Download and install based on your OS
b. Download Kali Linux ISO
Go to: https://www.kali.org/get-kali/⁠�
Download the Installer ISO or VirtualBox image
3. Install VirtualBox
Run the installer
Follow on-screen instructions
Install all required drivers
Launch VirtualBox after installation
4. Create a New Virtual Machine
Open VirtualBox
Click New
Enter:
Name: Kali Linux
Type: Linux
Version: Debian (64-bit)
Click Next
5. Allocate Memory (RAM)
Minimum: 2048 MB
Recommended: 4096 MB or more
6. Create Virtual Hard Disk
Select Create a virtual hard disk now
Choose:
File type: VDI (VirtualBox Disk Image)
Storage: Dynamically allocated
Set size:
Minimum: 20 GB
Recommended: 40–50 GB
7. Attach Kali Linux ISO
Select your VM → Click Settings
Go to Storage
Under Controller IDE:
Click Empty disk icon
Choose Kali Linux ISO file
Click OK
8. Configure Additional Settings (Recommended)
a. Processor
Go to System → Processor
Set:
2 CPUs (or more if available)
b. Display
Go to Display
Set Video Memory to 128 MB
c. Network
Default: NAT (works fine)
9. Start Installation
Click Start
Kali boot menu appears
Select:
Graphical Install
10. Install Kali Linux
Follow installer steps:
Select language, region, keyboard layout
Configure network (auto DHCP)
Set:
Hostname: kali
Username & Password
Disk partition:
Choose Guided – use entire disk
Confirm installation
11. Install GRUB Bootloader
Select Yes
Choose the default disk
12. Complete Installation
Wait for installation to finish
Remove ISO when prompted
Restart VM
13. Install Guest Additions (Important)
Start Kali VM
In VirtualBox menu:
Devices → Insert Guest Additions CD
Open terminal and run:
Bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt install build-essential dkms linux-headers-$(uname -r)
sudo mkdir /media/cdrom
sudo mount /dev/cdrom /media/cdrom
cd /media/cdrom
sudo sh VBoxLinuxAdditions.run
Reboot:
Bash
sudo reboot
14. Verify Installation
Login with your credentials
Check:
Full-screen mode works
Clipboard sharing (optional)
15. Optional Enhancements
Enable Shared Clipboard
Enable Drag and Drop
Take a Snapshot for backup
